# Sound effects in a notebook

## Section

```elixir
# The packages are siblings in the Cauldron checkout. With them on Hex,
# `{:cauldron_sfx_kino, "~> 0.4"}` is all this cell needs.
# If `__DIR__` is not the notebook's folder in your Livebook, put the absolute path in
# `package` instead.
package = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
cauldron = Path.expand("..", package)

Mix.install([
  {:cauldron_core, path: Path.join(cauldron, "cauldron_core")},
  {:cauldron_sfx_kino, path: package}
])
```

> **After changing the packages**, restart the runtime (or re-run this cell with
> "reinstall dependencies") — a path dependency is compiled once into Livebook's runtime,
> and an edit on disk does not reach a running one.

## What this notebook is

A game's sound effects are a table: an event name the game plays, and the sound it makes.
`Cauldron.SFX` is that table as data — a bank, `sfx.json` — and `Cauldron.Audio` uses it
unchanged:

```elixir
# bank = Cauldron.SFX.read!("priv/sfx/sfx.json")
# Cauldron.Audio.start(sounds: Cauldron.SFX.sounds(bank))
# Cauldron.Audio.play([:fire])
```

The cell below edits one. The same editor runs in a terminal (`mix cauldron.sfx`) and in a
browser (`mix cauldron.sfx.web`); all three drive `Cauldron.SFX.Editor`, the state machine
in `cauldron_core`, and nothing else.

## The editor

This is a widget, not a smart cell: run the cell and the editor appears under it. (A smart
cell — one you add from Livebook's insert menu — is a different thing, and this package
does not have one yet.)

Click an event to select it, or use the arrows. **play** renders the sound and plays it in
the cell — it is the game's own `Cauldron.Audio.Voice`. The buttons shape it:

|                                              |                                                  |
| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `shape`, `pitch`, `decay`, `sweep`, `cutoff` | the tone itself                                  |
| `attack`, `release`                          | how it starts and ends                           |
| `+` `-`, `pan`                               | the event's level and place                      |
| `space`                                      | a cheap room: the sound again, later and quieter |
| `variant +` / `variant -`                    | another take of the same event, or one fewer     |
| `preset`                                     | what a new event starts from                     |
| `delete event`, `write`                      | the bank                                         |

Type a name under the list and **add event** to make a new one; it starts as the preset in
the summary line. The name is the event the game plays, so `Cauldron.Audio.play([:laser])`
needs an event called `laser`. **write** writes `sfx.json`, in the notebook's working
directory.

```elixir
Cauldron.SFX.Kino.Editor.new()
```

## Hear every preset, without the widget

This is the quickest way to judge the sound: each preset rendered to a WAV and handed to
`Kino.Audio`, one tab each.

```elixir
tabs =
  for {name, %{recipe: recipe, space: space}} <- Cauldron.SFX.Presets.all() do
    try do
      bank =
        Cauldron.SFX.new()
        |> Cauldron.SFX.put_event(name, %{variants: [recipe], space: space})

      wav = bank |> Cauldron.SFX.voice(name) |> Cauldron.SFX.Audio.wav()

      {name, Kino.Audio.new(wav, :wav)}
    rescue
      error ->
        {name, Kino.Markdown.new("could not render: #{Exception.message(error)}")}
    end
  end

Kino.Layout.tabs(tabs)
```

`clang` plays a recording from `TuningFork.Kit`, so it is fetched the first time and needs
network once; the rest are synthesised.

## A recipe that plays a recording

A variant of kind `sample` plays a recording instead of a tone. `sound` names one of the
kit's hits; `file` is a path, which works offline.

```elixir
Cauldron.SFX.from_map(%{
  "events" => %{
    "clank" => %{
      "space" => 0.3,
      "variants" => [%{"kind" => "sample", "sound" => "hh", "gain" => 0.7}]
    }
  }
})
|> Cauldron.SFX.event("clank")
```

## If the widget comes up blank, or silent

The widget draws itself with JavaScript and plays through an `<audio>` element, so a
browser that blocks the sound leaves the controls there to press. If the cell itself is
empty, this plays the same sound with nothing but Elixir and `Kino.Audio`:

```elixir
bank = Cauldron.SFX.starter()
wav = bank |> Cauldron.SFX.voice("fire", variant: 0) |> Cauldron.SFX.Audio.wav()

Kino.Audio.new(wav, :wav, autoplay: true)
```

## Start from a bank file instead

`new/1` takes a path, so a game's own bank opens here with the effects it already has:

```elixir
# Cauldron.SFX.Kino.Editor.new("priv/sfx/sfx.json")
```

A layered event — `explosion` among the presets — is two recipes playing together. Its
parts are shown but not edited one by one; the whole event's gain, pan, space and variants
are.

## What the bank holds

An event is a gain, a pan, a space, a priority and its variants. A variant is a recipe: a
tone, a noise, layers of them, or a recording.

```elixir
bank = Cauldron.SFX.starter()

bank
|> Cauldron.SFX.events()
|> Enum.map(fn name -> {name, length(Cauldron.SFX.event(bank, name).variants)} end)
```

The recipe behind one of them:

```elixir
Cauldron.SFX.variant(bank, "fire", 0)
```

And the sound that recipe makes:

```elixir
Cauldron.SFX.voice(bank, "fire", variant: 0)
```

## In the game

```elixir
# In the game's start-up:
#   bank = Cauldron.SFX.read!("priv/sfx/sfx.json")
#   Cauldron.Audio.start(sounds: Cauldron.SFX.sounds(bank))
#
# Wherever the event happens:
#   Cauldron.Audio.play([:fire])
#
# An event the game varies — {:bomb, by} — stays in the game's own sounds/0: a bank names
# sounds, and a compound event is the table's business.
```
